# The curly family as one movement: c, o, a, d and g all begin with c's curl, and each is finished
# without lifting the pencil.
lesson writing-a-letter-family v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=2 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A letter family"
  goal "Write the letters that start the same way, c, o, a, d and g, each from one movement."

  look {
    scene 34x14 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=end {
        formletter c letter="c" copies=0 write=0 label=true
        formletter a letter="a" copies=0 write=0 label=true
        formletter d letter="d" copies=0 write=0 label=true
        formletter g letter="g" copies=0 write=0 label=true
      }
    }
    say """
      At the printing works Tia sorts the metal letters into families. These four, and o, all
      start the same way: near the top, curling round to the left like the start of a circle.
      That is c. Keep going round and up and you have o; close it and go down and you have a; go
      up tall first and you have d; go down past the line and hook back and you have g. One
      movement each, without lifting the pencil.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked writing.starts-with-curl k=0 p=0
    }
    practice writing.starts-with-curl count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice writing.curl-trace count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice writing.movement-parts count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice writing.curl-count count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice writing.wrong-start count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show writing.first-stroke k=7 easy-k=6 hard-k=8
    level hard {
      show writing.movement-parts ch="a" p=2 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show writing.letter-tray n=12 c=5 w=0 easy-n=8 easy-c=3 hard-n=16 hard-c=9 hard-w=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.tells-apart k=1 r=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.curl-word k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "c, o, a, d and g start like c: near the top, round to the left, in one movement."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that five letters are one movement with different endings, so a child who can
    start c can start all of them, and the thing to watch is the start, not the finished
    shape. The look is the four letters with their dots and arrows. The way in asks which of
    three letters starts with the curl, read from the dots. The core traces and writes the
    family (yours to mark: watch that the pencil starts at the dot, goes left first, and does
    not lift), orders the parts of one letter's movement, counts the curly letters in a row,
    finds the letter Tia started in the wrong place, and asks which way the pencil goes first.
    The wrong idea to watch for is starting d with its tall stroke, or a with an upward stroke
    from the line; both look fine at this size and come apart when the writing joins up. The
    story counts the tray at the printing works, the two-star question asks which part of the
    movement tells two look-alikes apart, and the three-star question is a word from the family
    written for you to mark. Short bursts: five or six of one letter, then something else.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the curly letter among three is worked once in the teacher's pen before o and a among straight letters, the letters to trace are c, o and a, the movement to order is a or d nearly in order, the row to count has three curly letters among five, the wrong start is a letter started at the bottom, the first stroke is c's, the tray has eight letters, the look-alikes are a and d, and the word to write is dog."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the curly letter hides beside h and b or y and p, the letters to trace are d and g, the movement to order is g reversed with a second, a, in the core, the row to count has four curly letters among five, the wrong start is d begun with its tall stroke, the first stroke is d's, the tray has sixteen letters and asks what is left, the look-alikes are a and g, and the word is the child's own from the five letters."
  }
}
